A||||11||Patrick||On a serious note, a moment of silence...||||1
Z||000000||Patrick||10-31-1999||08:03 PM||||<BR>I don't know if this is proper or not, but I'd just like to take a moment to show my respect to Indy race car driver, <B>Greg Moore</B> who died in a horrible crash today. He was only 24 years old.<P>Greg died doing something that he loved, racing cars. As this is an automotive forum, I think that there's probably a lot of us here who also would have loved to have been race car drivers if we had been given half a chance.<P>Greg Moore realized that he was in the public spotlight all of the time, and that he had the power to influence a whole generation of young Indy fans. He handled the responsibility exceptionally well and always acted accordingly. Greg was from my part of the world here in British Columbia. His family still lives in this area. He was obviously very popular here when he returned home to participate in the Vancouver Indy race.<P><BR>There'll be just one last trip home now.<P><BR>Forgive me, this sounds really corny, but maybe give someone you love a hug. You just never know if you'll get another chance.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Patrick (edited 11-01-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||kel||10-31-1999||08:39 PM||||I told a friend once, none of us really knows how long we have on this earth...but if we let the people we love and care about know how we feel while we're here, there's a kind of peace in that, don't you think?||||reg||1
Z||000002||four50four||10-31-1999||09:23 PM||||wow, thats pretty major. at least he died doing what he loved. wow, i'm still in shock.||||reg||9
Z||000003||Boxdin||10-31-1999||11:07 PM||||A sad day   He was a fine man||||reg||9
Z||000004||Dan||11-01-1999||06:47 AM|||| Living in Indy, I'm a real CART fan,po Tony George and the IRL.Any Cart Fan had the ultimate respect for Greg Moore and his team. Greg was one of my favorites and he will be sorely missed. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eric||11-01-1999||08:53 AM||||It's too bad he died so young.  I think it was obvious at the age of 24 he could have been one of the greatest racers of all time.  I didn't watch the race, but I did see the crash and I did hear the qualifying speeds.  It seems like these guys are out there taunting death running up to 250mph I think it was really only a matter of time.  It really sucks that CART hasn't figured this out.  I wonder if this event could trigger a union between IRL and CART.  IRL cars are much slower and seem to be as safe or safer for the drivers and now for the fans because of the wheel restraints.  Track and course design should be questioned too.  It always takes a major incident to get the track owners to fix a section that is a danger to the drivers.  I just hope something can be done.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||11-01-1999||09:36 AM||||I was watching the race when Greg crashed and got that terrible empty-stomach-pit feeling I've had so many times before when a driver takes a big hit.  But this time, there was no sigh of relief as the driver crawls from the wreckage and waves to the crowd.<P>I couldn't help but feel for Juan Montoya, who stood there looking crestfallen, saying, "He didn't deserve to die."<P>Greg will be sorely missed.||||reg||9
Z||000007||Archer||11-01-1999||10:51 PM||||I've been sitting here all evening reading about Greg and the racing community reaction.Years ago I spent a month in Methodist Hospital in Indianapolis recovering from spinal surgery caused by a racing accident.Dr.Terry Tramel came into my room the morning after surgery to scold me for walking the halls half the night.I thought that if I kept moving,I would heal faster and get back in the car sooner.He asked,"What's with you racers"<BR>  Ten years later I still can't answer that question in words that can be understood by someone who has not experienced this feeling.<BR>  My heart goes out to the Moore family.<BR>  Godspeed, Greg Moore||||reg||1
Z||000008||jhar1987||11-02-1999||12:06 AM||||we've lost two drivers this year. please its time to slow them down. i read where last year the indy cars were creating 4000lbs of down force,so cart changed the rules and this year they only have 2000lbs of down force. and they go faster now then before. how about a flat bottomed race car, should slow them down. we will miss you greg.....||||reg||1
Z||000009||deceler8||11-02-1999||03:10 PM||||While we're on the topic of fast and agile, the greatest running back ever passed away at the age of 45 yesterday.<P>The best I've ever seen and a great role model. Thanks for the fun, Walter.||||reg||9
Z||000010||ChadMan||11-02-1999||03:36 PM||||Not only was Walter arguably the best running back in the history of football (Brown? Sanders?), he was also a big race fan.  He was part-owner of a CART team and at one time drove in the SCCA Trans-Am series.  I never met him, but friends that have say he had a great sense of humor and always had time for his fans.<P>Moore, now Payton.  What a sad week to be a race fan.||||reg||9
Z||000011||DJRice||11-03-1999||08:35 AM||||Actually, its been a rough couple of weeks for several sports.  Payne Stewart died last week too.  He lived up in Orlando, so his death was a real big deal around here.||||reg||1
